WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:06.090
This is what's known
as the Svalbard Stroll.

00:00:06.090 --> 00:00:09.370
It's a brisk, hands-in-pockets
type of affair

00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:11.530
because you're at the end of the Earth

00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:13.850
and it's really, freaking cold.

00:00:13.850 --> 00:00:16.370
So this is the first time
you've ever seen this place?

00:00:16.370 --> 00:00:18.120
I've never been here before.

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:20.777
No, I never even thought I
would ever be in Svalbard.

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:24.900
My arctic companion is Nat Friedman,

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:28.210
CEO of the software platform GitHub.

00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:30.330
He usually lives in San Francisco,

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:32.490
but he's traveled to the ends of the Earth

00:00:32.490 --> 00:00:34.310
to do something peculiar.

00:00:34.310 --> 00:00:38.770
So, we're here in Svalbard,
at 78 degrees north latitude,

00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:43.770
at the site of the future
GitHub Arctic Code Vault.

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:46.960
He's depositing 6,000 of the most popular

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:51.046
open-source projects in an
archive inside this mountain.

00:00:51.046 --> 00:00:52.457
Open the vault!

00:00:54.623 --> 00:00:56.600
Nat's goal here is one,

00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:59.640
to protect the world's
software from the apocalypse,

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:03.130
and two, preserve our modern way of life.

00:01:03.130 --> 00:01:04.890
The man is ambitious.

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:09.600
How much of this is an
existential risk type thing?

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:11.430
Most of the time when you build a product

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:13.690
you build it hoping that lots
of people use it all the time,

00:01:13.690 --> 00:01:16.300
and this is probably a case
where we're building a product,

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:17.807
kind of hoping that it never gets used,

00:01:17.807 --> 00:01:19.017
in a way.

00:01:19.017 --> 00:01:21.220
Fingers crossed Nat

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:22.053
fingers crossed.

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:41.930
For those of you who haven't
wintered in Svalbard,

00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:44.743
it's located way up here.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:50.300
A frigid, barren wasteland, void of trees

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:52.800
or any meaningful vegetation.

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:55.080
The last spot humans can tolerate

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:58.270
before the Arctic proves too much.

00:01:58.270 --> 00:02:01.460
Getting here requires much
planning and deep resolve

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:03.403
to face Mother Nature at her worst.

00:02:05.570 --> 00:02:07.880
Or, you know, a private jet.

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:11.370
And the courage gained by
downing bubbly by the gallon,

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:13.463
and embracing your inner playboy.

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:17.177
Hello, Svalbard.

00:02:21.730 --> 00:02:24.600
Coal mining used to be
the main game in Svalbard

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:26.750
but since that's not cool anymore,

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:28.940
people here have had
to find some other uses

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:30.800
for all their permafrost,

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:34.450
which brings us back to our
coal mine, and our code cave.

00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:36.060
Is this is what you expected the entrance

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:37.610
to your seed vault to look like?

00:02:37.610 --> 00:02:39.948
It legitimately looks like
the entrance to a mine.

00:02:39.948 --> 00:02:41.130
So, I believe,

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:42.930
we are going deep inside a mountain.

00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:45.630
This is a old coal mine.

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:48.500
Nat and I were joined
by some proper mine men,

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:50.490
who taught us the ways of the mine.

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:52.448
And here you have some instrument?

00:02:52.448 --> 00:02:53.281
Oh, okay.

00:02:53.281 --> 00:02:54.430
Oh, to check the methane.

00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:56.130
And who were also helpful

00:02:56.130 --> 00:02:58.899
in firming up the concept
of existential risk.

00:02:58.899 --> 00:03:00.220
And when you product coal,

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:01.830
there will come a little bit of gas.

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:04.084
You don't feel it, you die.

00:03:04.084 --> 00:03:05.421
But you have apoxia.

00:03:05.421 --> 00:03:06.254
You die.

00:03:06.254 --> 00:03:07.500
Yeah, that sounds dangerous.

00:03:08.483 --> 00:03:10.310
With the safety brief down,

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:12.112
it's time to get on to the real business

00:03:12.112 --> 00:03:14.090
of protecting the world's code.

00:03:16.910 --> 00:03:18.210
This is how it works,

00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:20.110
the data is stored on a reel of film,

00:03:20.110 --> 00:03:21.983
coated with iron-oxide powder.

00:03:22.970 --> 00:03:25.900
The information can still
be read by a computer,

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:28.600
or if need be, by a human
with a magnifying glass.

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:30.210
How long will this last?

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:32.253
We are confident for 1,000

00:03:32.253 --> 00:03:34.480
and we're aiming now
to do research project

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:36.043
to document 2,000 years.

00:03:36.043 --> 00:03:37.300
2,000 years?

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:38.647
You think this could
last up to 2,000 years?

00:03:39.461 --> 00:03:40.440
We collected the film,

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:43.290
which is spooled inside
these white plastic cases,

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:45.480
and headed into the darkness.

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:50.066
I mean, usually I have the hard hats,

00:03:50.070 --> 00:03:51.559
and I feel like it's for show.

00:03:51.559 --> 00:03:53.000
Yeah, this is not for show.

00:03:53.169 --> 00:03:55.000
As we venture further into the abyss,

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:57.123
let me catch you up on who Nat is.

00:03:58.269 --> 00:04:01.080
Nat's company GitHub is
the main place people go

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:03.430
to write open source code.

00:04:03.430 --> 00:04:06.230
Tens of millions of people hop on GitHub

00:04:06.230 --> 00:04:09.630
and create the applications
that make the world tick.

00:04:09.630 --> 00:04:11.620
Which is why Nat wants to protect it

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:14.880
from terrorist hackers,
electromagnetic pulses,

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:17.010
and other unforeseen disasters.

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:18.540
Where are we going?

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:19.649
And where we were going,

00:04:19.649 --> 00:04:21.621
was not good.

00:04:21.621 --> 00:04:22.680
Okay.

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:24.840
Let's get the hell out.

00:04:25.052 --> 00:04:27.000
But like all intrepid explorers,

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:30.510
we would not let something
like a lack of oxygen stop us.

00:04:30.510 --> 00:04:34.880
And thank god for that,
because I now present to you

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:38.100
the most futuristic, ultra secure,

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:41.203
sci-fi inspired code
vault you will ever see.

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:48.780
Okay, it's basically a tool
shed, but it's still cool.

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:50.873
Wow.
Into the data vault.

00:04:52.390 --> 00:04:56.830
I mean, this is reel one, of
the GitHub Arctic Code Vault

00:04:56.830 --> 00:05:00.448
and we're gonna put it here,
in Svalbard, under the ice,

00:05:00.448 --> 00:05:02.255
for the next 2,000 years.

00:05:03.083 --> 00:05:04.505
Here we go.

00:05:05.930 --> 00:05:06.763
Thank you.

00:05:06.763 --> 00:05:08.720
Like, I think, 20 years ago,

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:11.230
if you'd told someone that 

00:05:11.230 --> 00:05:13.520
20 years in the future, in the year 2020,

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:16.410
all of human civilization
will depend on and run on

00:05:16.410 --> 00:05:19.070
open source code, written for free,

00:05:19.070 --> 00:05:21.460
and put into almost every
product in the world,

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:22.980
I think people would
say, like, that's crazy,

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:23.955
like, that's never gonna happen.

00:05:23.955 --> 00:05:25.450
You know software's written

00:05:25.450 --> 00:05:28.950
by big professional companies,
and yet, here we are.

00:05:28.950 --> 00:05:30.320
Yeah, and so how much of this

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:32.670
is just making sure we can
restore our way of life?

00:05:32.670 --> 00:05:34.890
I'm overall pretty optimistic
about civilization,

00:05:34.890 --> 00:05:36.870
like, I think we can bet that, you know,

00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:39.540
humans will be thriving for
a long time on planet Earth.

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:41.700
And so, another way to think about this,

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:43.310
is it's just like a time capsule.

00:05:43.310 --> 00:05:46.100
Like there's this amazing
moment in history where

00:05:46.100 --> 00:05:48.260
the whole world is starting
to run on software,

00:05:48.260 --> 00:05:51.160
and that software is
made out of open source

00:05:51.160 --> 00:05:53.383
and open source is sort of in everything.

00:05:57.180 --> 00:05:58.110
20 years ago,

00:05:58.110 --> 00:06:00.753
open source software was
seen as a fringe idea.

00:06:01.650 --> 00:06:04.230
The big companies kept their codes secret.

00:06:04.230 --> 00:06:06.140
Only weird hippie types shared code

00:06:06.140 --> 00:06:07.513
and gave it away for free.

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:11.957
Fast forward to 2019, and
about 40 million people

00:06:11.957 --> 00:06:15.033
and 2 million companies and
organizations use GitHub.

00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:20.800
That's why Microsoft paid
a stunning $7.5 billion

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:22.683
to acquire the company last year.

00:06:24.165 --> 00:06:26.344
I'll just leave it like this.

00:06:27.337 --> 00:06:27.950
Safe in

00:06:27.950 --> 00:06:29.490
the knowledge that open-source code

00:06:29.490 --> 00:06:33.680
will be secure, it was time
for me to explore Svalbard.

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:36.480
Nat and I returned to our nerd pursuits.

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:37.750
This time we were checking out

00:06:37.750 --> 00:06:40.060
the northernmost GitHub users

00:06:40.060 --> 00:06:41.980
who work at an observatory

00:06:41.980 --> 00:06:45.290
dedicated to researching
the Northern Lights.

00:06:45.290 --> 00:06:47.770
It was lunchtime, and we
needed to get there quick,

00:06:47.770 --> 00:06:49.460
before it got dark.

00:06:49.460 --> 00:06:51.220
1:20 p.m. sun is setting.

00:06:51.220 --> 00:06:54.070
It never really rose, actually,
we didn't see it all day.

00:06:56.300 --> 00:06:57.980
Here in this observatory

00:06:57.980 --> 00:06:59.780
perched on a snowy mountain,

00:06:59.780 --> 00:07:02.570
work some seriously hardy scientists.

00:07:02.570 --> 00:07:05.100
So, during the daytime, for
three and a half months,

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:07.540
over the winter, it is completely dark.

00:07:07.540 --> 00:07:11.400
And so, we can make 24 hour
observations of the Aurora.

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:14.320
And why would somebody
want to study the Aurora?

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:16.820
The Aurora is important for understanding

00:07:16.820 --> 00:07:19.530
the whole process of
the connection between

00:07:19.530 --> 00:07:22.080
the sun and the Earth's magnetic field,

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:24.850
and then the ultimate
impact on the atmosphere.

00:07:24.850 --> 00:07:27.150
So, this is what we call space weather.

00:07:29.371 --> 00:07:30.410
This arctic island

00:07:30.410 --> 00:07:32.085
is full of surprises.

00:07:32.085 --> 00:07:34.850
I have to do a photo, man, this is crazy.

00:07:34.850 --> 00:07:37.090
There are a lot of white mountains,

00:07:37.090 --> 00:07:40.610
some more white mountains,
and these white mountains,

00:07:40.610 --> 00:07:42.010
which host one of the world's

00:07:42.010 --> 00:07:44.093
biggest satellite ground stations.

00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:48.510
Our modern infrastructure relies

00:07:48.510 --> 00:07:50.133
on the messages sent from here.

00:07:53.300 --> 00:07:55.100
But if you need a break from nerding out,

00:07:55.100 --> 00:07:58.720
the real action takes place
in the town of Longyearbyen.

00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:01.890
Which is clearly named after
a very lonely coal miner,

00:08:01.890 --> 00:08:03.023
who'd had enough.

00:08:04.139 --> 00:08:05.530
As dumb luck would have it,

00:08:05.530 --> 00:08:08.450
I was in town during the
island's blues festival.

00:08:08.450 --> 00:08:11.580
An annual event that
brings in serious musicians

00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:14.020
and serves as one last drunken hooray

00:08:14.020 --> 00:08:16.360
before months of darkness set in.

00:08:16.360 --> 00:08:17.193
I mean,

00:08:17.193 --> 00:08:19.500
it's not so much the alcohol,
as it is the cold, that's...

00:08:19.500 --> 00:08:24.443
So I got drunk, and hoorahed.

00:08:26.020 --> 00:08:28.293
Then I got a truly horrible hangover.

00:08:31.390 --> 00:08:34.223
Had a rough night at the Blues Festival.

00:08:35.140 --> 00:08:38.661
Today I will feel better
by going dog sledding.

00:08:40.470 --> 00:08:43.441
You got the gun, for the,
uh, polar bear situation?

00:08:43.441 --> 00:08:45.784
Polar protection, yeah,
we have dogs with us, so,

00:08:45.784 --> 00:08:48.021
they're better protection
probably than a gun.

00:08:48.021 --> 00:08:48.865
Okay, okay.

00:08:59.857 --> 00:09:02.023
It's not quite the private jet but,

00:09:02.023 --> 00:09:04.803
it's my preferred way of
getting around Svalbard.

00:09:09.050 --> 00:09:10.310
As the trip neared it's end,

00:09:10.310 --> 00:09:12.550
we went to Svalbard's bizarrely amazing

00:09:12.550 --> 00:09:14.550
fine dining establishment to celebrate.

00:09:14.550 --> 00:09:18.490
Fried cod skin filled with
the Norwegian king crab.

00:09:18.490 --> 00:09:20.490
Things were going fine for a while.

00:09:20.490 --> 00:09:23.480
Fancy food, fancy booze,

00:09:23.480 --> 00:09:26.181
and the theme of our
journey got very real.

00:09:26.181 --> 00:09:28.936
Holy shit.
Wow, that's so crazy.

00:09:28.936 --> 00:09:29.850
It turns out,

00:09:29.850 --> 00:09:33.590
that about 4,000 miles
away in Sonoma California,

00:09:33.590 --> 00:09:35.720
Nat's house had been
destroyed by the fires

00:09:35.720 --> 00:09:37.650
raging through the state.

00:09:37.650 --> 00:09:39.840
It's super apropos to what
we're doing right now, right?

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:40.673
So wait,

00:09:40.673 --> 00:09:42.680
so somebody posted that on Twitter?

00:09:42.680 --> 00:09:44.900
He had the gross modern experience

00:09:44.900 --> 00:09:48.610
of seeing the remains of his
house end up on social media.

00:09:48.610 --> 00:09:51.170
Oh my gosh that's super devastating.

00:09:51.170 --> 00:09:52.050
So there we were,

00:09:52.050 --> 00:09:54.160
with parts of California burning,

00:09:54.160 --> 00:09:56.813
and Silicon Valley going
through rolling blackouts.

00:09:57.670 --> 00:10:00.900
The epicenter of technology
feeling very fragile,

00:10:00.900 --> 00:10:03.523
amid signs that the
world is not quite right.

00:10:05.230 --> 00:10:08.670
In that moment, the idea
of a remote code cave

00:10:08.670 --> 00:10:10.293
made all too much sense.

